  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a strong day at Port Washington but masked a deeper concern across the portfolio: capacity is sliding, and we're not staffing to match demand signals.\n\nPort Washington fired on all cylinders\u2014$3,844 revenue on six groomers ($484/groomer, 3% above baseline) with retail nearly tripling to $579. Week-to-date is up 18%. But month-to-date tells a different story: you're down 7% absolute, and even after DOW adjustment, you're 9.7% below where you should be. The calendar mix is real, but it's not the full story\u2014you've also run 90 groomer-days MTD versus 94 last month. Hicksville is up 15% MTD (beating DOW expectation by 4.6%) and you're now producing $424/groomer-day there, up from $385 last month. That's a genuine operational win. The issue: neither location has bathers scheduled today or tomorrow. Hicksville ran zero bather hours yesterday and has zero scheduled going forward\u2014that's capacity you're leaving on the table.\n\nGlen Cove is a bright spot on momentum (up 50% WTD, $228/groomer yesterday) but is completely dark today and tomorrow with no groomer-days scheduled for the next week. That's not a hold; that's a shutdown.\n\nThe pattern across all three: you're understaffed relative to demand, particularly on bathering, and Hicksville and Glen Cove need immediate scheduling attention. Port Washington's lapse pool is $318k in annual value\u2014that's your reactivation runway if you can staff to serve it.\n\nPort Washington today: 7 groomers, 15.0h bathers. Tomorrow: 6 groomers, 15.0h bathers. Next 7 days: 36 groomer-days across both flagship locations, 83h bathers\u2014but zero coverage at Glen Cove."
